<h2>How your policy handles wind damage claims in San Marcos</h2>

<h3>No TWIA in Hays County</h3>

<p>Coastal Texas counties rely on the <a href="https://www.twia.org/about-us/overview/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Windstorm Insurance Association</a> for windstorm coverage. Hays County is not in that area. For San Marcos, Kyle, Buda, and Wimberley, wind coverage normally sits inside your regular homeowners or commercial property policy, or the Texas FAIR Plan in some cases. That also means TDI windstorm (WPI-8) certification rules for the coast do not apply here.</p>

<h3>The wind/hail deductible</h3>

<p>Many Texas policies carry a separate deductible for wind and hail, often a percentage of the dwelling limit. On a $300,000 Coverage A limit, a 1% deductible is $3,000 and a 2% deductible is $6,000. Some policies also include roof payment schedules or actual cash value (ACV) roof endorsements that reduce payment on older roofs. Check your declarations page, and see our guide to <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value</a> if you are unsure how your roof will be valued.</p>

<h3>Rain that follows the wind</h3>

<p>Many policies cover interior water damage from rain only if wind or hail first created an opening in the roof or walls. That makes it important to document the opening itself, such as missing shingles or a peeled panel, alongside the stained ceiling.</p>

<h2>When a tree falls: what is usually covered</h2>

<p>Fallen trees are one of the most common wind claims around San Marcos, where mature live oaks, elms, and cedars shade many older neighborhoods and Hill Country lots. Coverage depends on what the tree hits and on your policy wording, but the general pattern looks like this:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Tree hits the house or a covered structure.</strong> Damage to the structure is typically covered as a wind loss, subject to the deductible. Many policies also pay a limited amount to remove the tree from the structure.</li>
<li><strong>Tree hits a fence, shed, or detached garage.</strong> Usually handled under "other structures" coverage, which has its own limit.</li>
<li><strong>Tree falls in the yard and hits nothing covered.</strong> Removal is often not covered, or covered only in narrow situations described in the policy, such as a tree blocking a driveway.</li>
<li><strong>Neighbor's tree hits your home.</strong> Your own policy usually handles damage to your property. Questions about a neighbor's responsibility are liability questions; talk with your insurer or a Texas attorney.</li>
</ul>

<p>Before cutting anything, photograph the tree where it landed, the root ball or broken trunk, and the damage underneath. Then take reasonable steps to prevent further damage, like tarping, and keep every receipt.</p>

<h2>How to document a wind damage claim</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Pin down the date of loss.</strong> Note when the wind hit. Our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a> can help you match your address and date to official reports.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph everything before cleanup.</strong> Wide shots of each side of the building, then close-ups. Capture debris in the yard, too; shingles and panels on the ground tell a story.</li>
<li><strong>Protect the property.</strong> Tarp roof openings and board windows if it is safe. Most policies require reasonable steps to prevent further damage.</li>
<li><strong>Save damaged materials when practical.</strong> A few pieces of torn shingle or panel can help show the type and age of the materials.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim promptly</strong> and keep a written log of calls, emails, and inspection dates.</li>
<li><strong>Keep receipts</strong> for emergency repairs, tree work, and any extra living expenses if the home is not livable.</li>
</ol>

<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> walks through these steps in more detail.</p>

<h2>Wind damage claim steps after the inspection</h2>

<p>After you report the claim, the insurer will usually send an adjuster to inspect and then issue an estimate. Under the Texas Prompt Payment of Claims Act, insurers generally must acknowledge a claim and begin their investigation within 15 days of notice, and accept or reject it within 15 business days after receiving the information they requested, with extensions available in some situations, including declared weather catastrophes. The <a href="https://www.tdi.texas.gov/consumer/storms/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Texas Department of Insurance</a> has more on what to expect.</p>

<p>Compare the insurer's estimate line by line against what you documented. Wind claims are often underpaid because the scope covers only the most visible spots, overlooks creased shingles or loose fasteners, or ignores matching issues on siding and metal panels. If the numbers do not add up, you can provide additional documentation, request a reinspection, or ask about the appraisal clause for disputes over the amount of loss. Our page on <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a> covers those options.</p>

<h2>How a San Marcos public adjuster can help</h2>

<p>A licensed <a href="/">San Marcos public adjuster</a> represents you, the policyholder, rather than the insurance company. For a wind claim, that can mean reviewing your policy and deductible, inspecting the full property, building a detailed estimate, and handling communication with the carrier so you can focus on repairs. We cannot guarantee a result; every claim depends on the damage and the policy terms. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins, and Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

